{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChLKJ9NVgbgse45RWmxn2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed both problems in `sympy/printing/mathematica.py`, and the existing Mathematica printer tests pass (9 passed).\n\n**Derivatives.** `Derivative(f(t), t)` now prints as `Hold[D[f[t], t]]`. I wrapped it in `Hold[...]` so Mathematica doesn't evaluate it straight away, which is how this printer already handles `Integral` and `Sum`. That differs from the plain `D[f[t],t]` the issue suggests. If you want the bare form, just drop the `Hold[`. A repeated derivative prints each variable separately: `Derivative(sin(x), x, 2)` gives `Hold[D[Sin[x], x, x]]`. Mathematica accepts that, though `{x, 2}` would be the shorter way to write it.\n\n**Floats.** Floats written with an exponent now use Mathematica's `*^` notation: `1e-30` prints as `1.00000000000000*^-30` and `1e40` as `1.00000000000000*^+40`. `1.0e-4` itself prints as `0.0001`, because SymPy doesn't write that value with an exponent in the first place.\n\nI didn't use `str(expr)` as the issue suggested, because it broke an existing test: it turned `3.5` into `3.50000000000000`. Instead the new method keeps the printer's existing float output and only replaces `e` with `*^`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1272,"cache_read_input_tokens":21832,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1272},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e1c63b0d-cfc8-4229-94ba-fef2ded25e39","uuid":"31536f7e-0b42-46dd-984e-05e259cc00e6","timestamp":"2026-11-23T18:15:45.040Z","request_id":"req_011ChLKJ98bkNX2JxWTDcDRd"}
